rem 5.3.\nL575: \nL576: Let $\\mathcal{T},\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}\\in\\tors\\Lambda$. Then $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$ if and only if both of the following hold.\nL577: \nL578:   1. (1)\nL579: \nL580: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})\\subseteq\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})$.\nL581: \nL582:   2. (2)\nL583: \nL584: For every $X\\in\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$ admitting a surjection $Z\\twoheadrightarrow X$.\nL585: Moreover, if $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\downarrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})=\\mathcal{T}$, then $(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$.\nL586: \nL587: Dually, $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}$ if and only if both of the following hold.\nL588: \nL589:   1. (1)\nL590: \nL591: There is an inclusion $\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})\\subseteq\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$.\nL592: \nL593:   2. (2)\nL594: For every $Y\\in\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime})$, there exists $Z\\in\\Filt(\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))$ admitting an injection $X\\hookrightarrow Z$.\nL595: \nL596: Moreover, if $\\mathrm{pop}^{\\uparrow}_{\\tors\\Lambda}(\\mathcal{T})=\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}$, then $(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}))=\\mu_{\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T})}(\\mathcal{D}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}),\\mathcal{U}(\\mathcal{T}^{\\prime}))$.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPop-Stack Operators for Torsion Classes and Cambrian Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2312.03959v1)\nciteturn9543view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn9537view1\",\"lineno\":1330}); Total lines: 1931\nL1328: At this point in time, there may be some partial arcs that were produced in the previous $k-1$ steps. If $k\\geq 2$ and $\\mathtt{M}_{k-1}\\neq\\mathtt{U}$, then we extend one of these partial arcs and attach it to the point $k$ in order to create an arc, and we extend the rest of the partial arcs above (if $k$ is odd) or below (if $k$ is even) the point $k$. The condition that arcs cannot cross uniquely determines which partial arc we must attach to the point $k$.\nL1329: During the same $k$-th step, if $k\\leq n$ and $\\mathtt{M}_{k+1}\\neq\\mathtt{D}$, then we create a new small partial arc that starts at the point $k$.\nL1330: To see that the above procedure succeeds in producing a noncrossing arc diagram in $\\mathrm{AD}(c^{\\times})$, we must check two things:\nL1331: \nL1332:   * •\nL1333: \nL1334: If $k\\geq 2$ and $\\mathtt{M}_{k-1}\\neq\\mathtt{U}$, then there is actually a partial arc from the first $k-1$ steps that we can attach to the point $k$.\nL1335: \nL1336:   * •\nL1337: \nL1338: The total number of steps during which we create a new partial arc is equal to the total number of steps during which we attach a partial arc to a point to create an arc.\nL1339: For the first item, note that the number of partial arcs remaining after the first $k-1$ steps is $(k-1-\\#_{\\mathtt{D}}(\\mathtt{M}_{2}\\cdots\\mathtt{M}_{k}))-(k-2-\\#_{\\mathtt{U}}(\\mathtt{M}_{1}\\cdots\\mathtt{M}_{k-2}))=\\#_{\\mathtt{U}}(\\mathtt{M}_{1}\\cdots\\mathtt{M}_{k-2})-\\#_{\\mathtt{D}}(\\mathtt{M}_{2}\\cdots\\mathtt{M}_{k})+1$.\nL1340: Because $M$ is a Motzkin path, this quantity is nonnegative; furthermore, it can only be $0$ if the steps $\\mathtt{M}_{k-1}$ and $\\mathtt{M}_{k}$ are $\\mathtt{U}$ and $\\mathtt{D}$ (respectively) and form a peak of height $1$. But $M$ has no peaks of height $1$, so the number of partial arcs remaining after $k-1$ steps must actually be at least $1$. The second bulleted item follows from the fact that $\\#_{\\mathtt{U}}(M)=\\#_{\\mathtt{D}}(M)$.\nL1341: Let us now show that the noncrossing arc diagram $\\delta$ constructed through the above procedure is actually maximal in $\\mathrm{AD}(c^{\\times})$; it will then follow immediately from the construction that $\\Psi(\\delta)=M$. Note also that the steps outlined above for constructing $\\delta$ were forced upon us; in other words, $\\Psi$ is injective.\nL1342: Let $\\mathfrak{a}_{i,j}$ denote the unique $c^{\\times}$-sortable arc from $i$ to $j$. Suppose by way of contradiction that there exist $i<j$ such that $\\mathfrak{a}_{i,j}$ is not in $\\delta$ and $\\delta\\sqcup\\{\\mathfrak{a}_{i,j}\\}$ is a noncrossing arc diagram. We will assume $i$ is even; the case when $i$ is odd is almost identical. We consider three cases.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Pop-Stack Operator on Ornamentation Lattices (https://arxiv.org/html/2501.10311v1)\nciteturn9543view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn9537view2\",\"lineno\":40}); Total lines: 456\nL36: Each rooted plane tree $\\mathsf{T}$ has an associated ornamentation lattice $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$. The ornamentation lattice of an $n$-element chain is the $n$-th Tamari lattice. We study the pop-stack operator $\\mathsf{Pop}\\colon\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})\\to\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, which sends each element $\\delta$ to the meet of the elements covered by or equal to $\\delta$.\nL37: We compute the maximum size of a forward orbit of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, generalizing a result of Defant for Tamari lattices. We also characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}$ on $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$, generalizing a result of Hong for Tamari lattices. For each integer $k\\geq 0$, we provide necessary conditions for an element of $\\mathcal{O}(\\mathsf{T})$ to be in the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$.\nL38: This allows us to completely characterize the image of $\\mathsf{Pop}^{k}$ on a Tamari lattice.\nL39: ## 1. Introduction\nL40: ### 1.1. Ornamentation Lattices\nL41: Laplante-Anfossi [cite20†15 ] recently introduced remarkable polytopes called operahedra. These polytopes are special examples of graph associahedra [cite21†5 , cite22†17 ] and poset associahedra [cite23†13 ], and they generalize both the associahedra and permutohedra. Laplante-Anfossi also considered certain posets obtained by orienting the $1$-skeletons of operahedra, and he asked if these posets are lattices.\nL42: Defant and Sack [cite24†11 ] answered Laplante-Anfossi’s question in the affirmative, naming his posets operahedron lattices. A key idea that Defant and Sack employed was to embed an operahedron lattice into the product of an interval in the weak order on the symmetric group and another lattice. This other lattice, which is the primary focus of this article, is a new structure called an ornamentation lattice.\nL43: Let $\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$ denote the set of rooted plane trees with $n$ nodes. Let $\\mathsf{T}\\in\\mathrm{PT}_{n}$. We view $\\mathsf{T}$ both as a graph and as a poset in which every non-root element is covered by exactly $1$ element (so the root is the unique maximal element). We denote the partial order on $\\mathsf{T}$ by $\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}$. For $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, we write\nL44:  | $$\\Delta_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)=\\{v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathsf{T}:v^{\\prime}\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}v\\}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\nabla_{\\mathsf{T}}(v)=\\{v^{\\prime}\\in\\mathsf{T}:v\\leq_{\\mathsf{T}}v^{\\prime}\\}.$$  |\nL45: An ornament of $\\mathsf{T}$ is a set of nodes that induces a connected subgraph of $\\mathsf{T}$. Let $\\mathrm{Orn}(\\mathsf{T})$ denote the set of ornaments of $\\mathsf{T}$. We will often view an ornament as a rooted plane tree. Two sets are said to be nested if one is a subset of the other. An ornamentation of $\\mathsf{T}$ is a function $\\delta\\colon\\mathsf{T}\\to\\mathrm{Orn}(\\mathsf{T})$ such that\nL46: \nL47:   * •\nL48: \nL49: for every $v\\in\\mathsf{T}$, the unique maximal element of $\\delta(v)$ is $v$;\nL50: \nL51:   * •\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.10030v1)\nciteturn9543view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn9538view0\",\"lineno\":22}); Total lines: 702\nL16@P0: Building on Knuth’s stack-sorting algorithm [16], West’s ground-breaking work on stack\u0002sorting map on symmetric groups [24] inspired subsequent studies, including the reverse\u0002stack-sorting map [12] and the pop-stack-sorting map [3]. Recently, there has been\nL17@P0: considerable attention by combinatorialists on the pop-stack sorting map [1, 2, 7, 13, 20].\nL18@P0: For each complete meet-semilattice M, Defant defined an operator PopM that agrees with\nL19@P0: the pop-stack-sorting map when M is the weak order on Sn [8]. It is defined so that\nL20@P0: PopM sends an element to the meet of itself and all elements that it covers. By definition,\nL21@P0: M’s minimal element ˆ0 stays the same when PopM is applied. We say an element x is\nL22@P0: t-Pop-sortable if Popt\nL23@P0: M(x) = ˆ0.\nL24@P0: Pudwell and Smith [20] enumerated the number of 2-Pop-sortable elements in Sn under the\nL25@P0: weak order. Claesson and Guðmundsson [7] proved that for each fixed nonnegative integer\nL26@P0: t, the generating function that counts t-Pop-sortable elements in Sn is rational. Defant [9]\nL27@P0: established the analogous rationality result for the generating functions of t-Pop-sortable\nL28@P0: elements of type B and type Ae weak orders.\nL29@P0: Introduced in 1962, the n-th Tamari lattice Tamn consists of semilength-n Dyck paths\nL30@P0: (lattice paths from (0, 0) to (n, n) above the diagonal y = x) [23]; its partial order will\nL31@P0: be defined in Section 2. There are generalizations of the definition, most notably the\nL32@P0: m-Tamari lattices by Bergeron and Pr´eville-Ratelle [4] and the ν-Tamari lattices introduced\nL33@P0: Date: December 2021.\nL34@P0: 1\nL35@P0-1: arXiv:2201.10030v1 [math.CO] 25 Jan 20222 LETONG HONG\nL36@P1: by Pr´eville-Ratelle and Viennot [19]. Fundamental in algebraic combinatorics [17], the n-th\nL37@P1: Tamari lattice Tamn is also isomorphic to Avn(312), the lattice of 312-pattern-avoiding\nL38@P1: permutations under the weak order of Sn [5].\nL39@P1: In this paper, we study the Pop operator on Tamari lattices. Let ht(n) be the number of\nL40@P1: t-Pop-sortable elements in Tamn. A part of a conjecture by Defant [8] is that for every\nL41@P1: fixed t, the generating function P\nL42@P1: n≥1\nL43@P1: ht(n)z\nL44@P1: n\nL45@P1: is rational. We confirm this statement by\nL46@P1: giving the exact formula of the generating function:\nL47@P1: Theorem 1.1. Let ht(n) denote the number of t-Pop-sortable Dyck paths in the n-th\nL48@P1: Tamari lattice Tamn. Then\nL49@P1: X\nL50@P1: n≥1\nL51@P1: ht(n)z\nL52@P1: n =\nL53@P1: z\nL54@P1: 1 − 2z −\nL55@P1: Pt\nL56@P1: j=2 Cj−1z\nL57@P1: j\nL58@P1: ,\nL59@P1: where Cj are the Catalan numbers.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n07_Pallo.dvi (https://acta.bibl.u-szeged.hu/12796/1/Pallo_2006_ActaCybernetica.pdf)\nciteturn9543view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 12; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn9538view1\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 426\nL0@P0: Acta Cybernetica 17 (2006) 799–810.\nL1@P0: Rotational tree structures on binary trees and\nL2@P0: triangulations∗\nL3@P0: Jean Marcel Pallo†\nL4@P0: Abstract\nL5@P0: A rotation in a binary tree is a simple and local restructuring technique\nL6@P0: commonly used in computer science. We propose in this paper three restric\u0002tions on the general rotation operation. We study the case when only leftmost\nL7@P0: rotations are permitted, which corresponds to a natural flipping on polygon\nL8@P0: triangulations. The resulting combinatorial structure is a tree structure with\nL9@P0: the root as the greatest element. We exhibit an efficient algorithm for com\u0002puting the join of two trees and the minimum number of leftmost rotations\nL10@P0: necessary to transform one tree into the other.\nL11@P0: Keywords: Binary trees; Rotation; Distance; Lattice; Algorithms\nL12@P0: 1 Introduction\nL13@P0: Rotation is one of the most common operations for restructuring binary trees. It\nL14@P0: has the advantage of altering the depths of some of the nodes in the tree while\nL15@P0: preserving the symmetric order of all the nodes. Thus rotation is commonly used\nL16@P0: in a variety of algorithms for maintaining binary search trees with a good amortized\nL17@P0: behavior [10, 24, 28].\nL18@P0: The combinatorial properties of binary trees under the rotation operation have\nL19@P0: been studied for thirty years [27]. In [17] we have shown that a directed version\nL20@P0: of the rotation graph of binary trees with n nodes is a lattice, known as the nth\nL21@P0: Tamari lattice. This corresponds to the case when only left rotations are permitted\nL22@P0: in the binary tree transformation. Over the last ten years, Tamari lattices have\nL23@P0: often been used as examples to illustrate algebraic theories [1, 3, 16, 25].\nL24@P0: Initially, Tamari lattices were orderings of parenthesizations of words. But\nL25@P0: nowadays they can be described in other ways via the well-known bijections between\nL26@P0: families of Catalan combinatorial objects. A system that is isomorphic to Tamari\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/journals/JIS/VOL9/Mansour/mansour86.pdf)\nciteturn9543view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 13; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn9538view2\",\"lineno\":385}); Total lines: 1295\nL298@P4: Fj+1(x; q) =\nL299@P4: Fj (x; q) − 1\nL300@P4: xFj (x; q)\nL301@P4: ,\nL302@P4: for all j = 0, 1, . . . , k − 2. To give an explicit formula for Fk(x; q) we need t